From b55ea4becd4a1ad6460ec4f55a8254e6b97182bf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 23:23:25 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] state: every wholesale FTS page-freeing path asks the
 whole-file plan first [daveey]

On the hub's damage shape (a trigram b-tree page referenced twice), DROP, FTS5
'rebuild'/'delete-all' and draining a tree's rows free the shared page twice,
and SQLite later hands it to canonical data. The online recovery and the
one-shot rebuild already refused; the other paths did not.

One gate, SessionDB._fts_wholesale_free_allowed (hermes_state_fts_online), now
guards: the open-time source realign, the trigram source-exclusion migration,
the orphan shadow-table sweep, the missing-trigger 'rebuild' of an existing
index, rebuild_fts(), the optimize-storage truncation ('delete-all') and the
trash teardown. A plan naming the family detaches search (fail-open,
_fts_oneshot_failed) so the retry unlinks it online; a refused plan changes
nothing. Runtime callers plan before self._lock (_fts_runtime_plan, one call
only); a gated path reached under the lock without a plan skips. Open-time
init plans inline once per open, and only when a gated operation would run.

Trash the online recovery creates is recorded in state_meta fts_trash_verified
in the same transaction and drains without a new plan; other trash (legacy
demotion, CJK retirement) drains only after a clean plan records it, and is
left alone when the check names it.

Three test setups that truncate the index under db._lock now make the plan
first (the new contract for 'delete-all').

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
